  "evidence": "B: Mandatory National Service; 8,000 more neighbourhood police and tougher sentences; legal annual cap on migration and monthly Rwanda removal flights; defence spending to 2.5% of GDP and support for Trident; welfare reform with tougher sanctions and a new Fraud Bill; tax cuts framed as rewarding work; protect single‑sex spaces and define sex in law as biological; protect the Union. A: Wealth tax and major redistribution (UBI pathway, benefit uplifts, rent controls); repeal Prevent and the ‘hostile environment’; humane asylum with safe routes and right to work; cancel Trident, reform NATO toward de‑escalation, sign Nuclear Ban Treaty; proportional representation and votes at 16; extensive public ownership and rights expansions.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile places the greatest weight on authority, proportionality, and then loyalty, with equality relatively lower. Text B concentrates on authority (national service, policing, borders, defence), proportionality (rewarding work, welfare sanctions, anti‑fraud), and national loyalty/union, whereas Text A foregrounds equality/redistribution, civil‑liberties expansion, and demilitarization, so B aligns more closely with the profile.",
